and relatives) is a.25.1.2 (SUNID 47253), and manganese catalase (T-catalase) is a.25.1.3 (SUNID 100951). Other families in the same superfamily include YciF-like, half-ferritin, PMT1231-like, MiaE-like, AMB4284-like and Rv2844-like. SCOPe therefore treats them as remotely homologous, meaning a shared evolutionary origin inferred from the conserved four-helix-bundle core and a conserved dimetal-binding site (carboxylate/His ligands, EXXH motifs). It does not treat them as close relatives with shared function. The family level groups closer, clearly related sequences. The superfamily level groups remote homologs that have low sequence identity but shared structure and often shared features. The fold level (a.25) only describes similar architecture. Here the superfamily and fold nodes are the same group, so the superfamily assignment goes beyond mere topological similarity.\\n\\nThe chemistries differ because the shared scaffold was adapted to different jobs. Ferritin is a ferroxidase and iron-storage protein: Fe(II) is oxidized by O2 or H2O2 at a dinuclear site, and the iron is mineralized in a 24-mer shell. RNR R2 forms a diiron-tyrosyl radical cofactor (Fe(III)2-Y•) that starts radical-based nucleotide reduction, and some members use Mn/Fe or Mn(III)2-Y•. Manganese catalase has a dimanganese site that disproportionates H2O2 by cycling between Mn(II)2 and Mn(III)2 (and higher states), with no radical or storage function. Different metals, redox cycles, quaternary structures and substrates evolved on the common scaffold. This is functional divergence within a superfamily, not conservation.\\n\\nLimits on interpretation: (1) A shared superfamily supports structural and evolutionary inference, such as the helical core and the presence of a metal site. It does not support transferring enzymatic function, metal specificity, substrate, or mechanism from one family to another. (2) Do not use a ferritin, RNR or catalase structure as a template for the others without checkin